course: dictionary
lesson: 14032
title: Mangled: when something is spoilt
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Mangled means badly crushed, twisted or torn, so that it is spoilt. You can see the clue in the sentence: «The bike was mangled after the crash with the lorry.» A crash is bad, so mangled must mean damaged. Swap in a meaning, such as «The bike was spoilt after the crash», and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Mangled can also describe words or music that are spoilt by being said or played very badly. «Alice mangled her poem, and the lines came out all wrong.» Alice did not tear the paper. She said the words badly, so the poem was spoilt.
explain: Mangle is also an old word for a machine with rollers that squeezed water out of washing. In Victorian times, wet sheets were pushed through a mangle to dry them. This is an old meaning, so you will mostly see mangled used for damage or for spoilt words.
fact: Mangled is an adjective when it describes a noun: a mangled car, a mangled poem.
fact: Mangle is a verb. Its past form is mangled: «She mangled the song.»
fact: «Mangled» means badly crushed, twisted or torn, so that it is spoilt.
fact: «Mangled» can also mean spoilt by being said or played very badly.
fact: In old times, a mangle was a machine with rollers that squeezed water out of washing.
exercise: «The car was mangled in the crash.» What does mangled mean here? | Very clean after a wash | *Badly crushed and spoilt | Freshly painted and shiny | Parked outside the house
exercise: «Alice mangled the words of her poem.» What does mangled mean here? | Sang the words very loudly | Wrote the words in gold ink | *Said so badly that the poem was spoilt | Read the words slowly and carefully
exercise: Find and copy the word in «The mangled bike lay in the road.» that tells you the bike was badly damaged. | bike | lay | *mangled | road
test:
In old times, the washing was pushed through a mangle to squeeze out the water. What does mangle mean in this old sense? | Badly crushed or torn | *A machine with rollers that squeezed water out of washing | Spoilt by being said badly | To sing a song well
«The singer mangled the national anthem.» What does this suggest? | She sang it beautifully | *She sang it so badly that it was spoilt | She sang it in another country | She forgot to sing it at all
exam: «The box was mangled, so the toys inside were broken.» What does mangled mean in this sentence? | Full of water | Painted red | *Badly crushed, twisted or torn | Brand new and shiny
